  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 329 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nA short wave will dive southeast through Alberta late Friday and \nphase with the low over the western U.S. early Saturday.  This forces \nlow level cyclogenesis over the northern Plains and the low moves \nalong the U.S./Canada border Friday night and farther northeast into \nCanada on Saturday.  The GFS and ECMWF are in good agreement on this \nsystem, and both models push the cold front into northwest Missouri \naround 00Z Sunday.  The combined upper level short wave continues \nmoving east, driving the surface front southeast through Missouri \nand Illinois on Sunday.  The ridge which has been blocking moisture \nreturn this week weakens as the wave moves through the Plains into \nthe Mississippi Valley on Sunday.  This allows moisture to flow \nnorth from the Gulf ahead of the front.  The GFS and ECMWF have been \nshowing some modest low level moisture convergence ahead of the \nfront on Saturday night and Sunday for the past few runs.  QPF \namounts have varied, but the trend has been upward and the latest \nruns of both models print out between 0.2 and 0.6 inches of rain for \nour area.  The NBM looks like it's finally catching up with global \nguidance and the LREF with 80-100 PoPs between 06Z-12Z Sunday across \ncentral and northeast Missouri into west central Illinois. PoPs have \nalso increased to 60+ percent between 12Z-18Z Sunday in eastern \nMissouri and southwest Illinois.  There's probably still room for \nimprovement between 12Z-18Z as 80-90 percent of the LREF members \nhave measurable QPF in that timeframe.  However, the probability for \n0.1 inch of rain in eastern Missouri and southwest Illinois is only \naround 40 percent according to the LREF, so some of the higher end \nQPF amounts look unlikely at this juncture.  Another cool high \npressure system builds into the Mississippi Valley behind the front \nearly next week. Temperatures look seasonably cool, very similar to \nlast weekend with highs in the 40s and lows in the 20s.",
